## Experiment
Topic: Topic Can the 120-split analysis be repeated on a genuinely untouched replication set under a time-stamped protocol that fixes the split count, grid, tie-breaking rules, regime check, bootstrap, robustness interval, and secondary analyses before any outcomes are inspected? _(auto-enqueued follow-up from topic-can-the-optimization-comparison-be-repeate)_
Goal: A preregistered synthetic classification experiment will compare permutation-invariant lexicographic tie-breaking with first-encountered-maximum tie-breaking across 60 untouched replication datasets, 25 fixed grid permutations, and 120 fixed evaluation splits.

Protocol to implement:
1. Use Python 3.11 with numpy==1.26.4, pandas==2.2.2, scikit-learn==1.4.2, and matplotlib==3.8.4; run single-threaded by setting OMP_NUM_THREADS=1, MKL_NUM_THREADS=1, and OPENBLAS_NUM_THREADS=1. 2. Before generating any arrays, write preregistration.json containing every constant and rule in this protocol, the implementation version, and the current UTC timestamp; write its SHA-256 digest to preregistration.sha256. No parameter may subsequently be changed. 3. Define a fixed grid of 12 deterministic configurations C00 through C11. Configuration Cj predicts the binary values in feature column X[:,j]. Label C00-C05 as optimizer-A and C06-C11 as optimizer-B. Define the reported optimization winner as the optimizer label of the selected configuration. Define the preregistered total order as ascending numeric configuration ID. 4. Generate 25 grid permutations once and reuse them for all datasets: for permutation k=0,...,24, compute numpy.random.default_rng(400000+k).permutation(12). Save the resulting integer matrix before generating data. 5. Generate 60 tuning datasets indexed d=0,...,59, each with n=400 observations and exactly 200 observations per class. Set y to 200 zeros followed by 200 ones and permute observation order with numpy.random.default_rng(100000+d). Define the four maximum-score configurations as T_d={(d+0) mod 12,(d+1) mod 12,(d+6) mod 12,(d+7) mod 12}. For each configuration j, independently within each class choose error positions without replacement using numpy.random.default_rng(110000+100*d+j). If j is in T_d, choose exactly 50 errors among each class's 200 observations, giving balanced accuracy 0.75; otherwise choose exactly 60 errors per class, giving balanced accuracy 0.70. Set X[:,j]=y XOR error_indicator. Do not fit a model or round scores. 6. Compute each configuration's tuning balanced accuracy as the unweighted mean of sensitivity and specificity. Run a pre-outcome regime check requiring, for every dataset, exactly four configurations tied at the exact maximum 0.75, all other configurations equal to 0.70, and the maximum set equal to T_d. Save the check results; any failure is an implementation-invalidating error, not grounds for altering the protocol. 7. For each dataset and each of the 25 permutations, select a configuration using two rules. Total-order rule: find the exact maximum tuning score and choose the tied configuration with the smallest numeric ID, regardless of encounter order. First-encountered rule: scan configurations in permutation order, initialize the first as incumbent, replace the incumbent only when score > incumbent_score, and never replace it when scores are equal. 8. Freeze all 60x25 selections before replication outcomes exist by saving selections.csv and its SHA-256 digest. Verify that no replication-generation function has yet been called. 9. Generate the untouched replication dataset for each d only after selections.csv is frozen. Use n=1200 observations with 600 zeros and 600 ones; permute their order using numpy.random.default_rng(200000+d). For configuration j, set q=(j+5*d) mod 12 and target balanced accuracy p=0.55+0.01*q. Independently for each class, choose exactly 600-(330+6*q) error positions without replacement using numpy.random.default_rng(210000+100*d+j), then set X_rep[:,j]=y_rep XOR error_indicator. Thus configurations have distinct full-replication balanced accuracies from 0.55 through 0.66, while replication outcomes cannot influence tuning or selection. 10. For each dataset, create exactly 120 shared replication splits with sklearn.model_selection.StratifiedShuffleSplit(n_splits=120,test_size=0.25,random_state=300000+d). Each test set must contain 300 observations, 150 per class. Apply every configuration to the same test indices and compute balanced accuracy on each split. Define a configuration's final 120-split estimate as the arithmetic mean of its 120 test balanced accuracies. Do not use training indices because configurations are fixed deterministic predictors. 11. For each rule, dataset, and permutation, report the selected configuration, optimizer label, and corresponding final 120-split estimate. Use unrounded float64 values for all comparisons and round only displayed tables to four decimal places. 12. For each dataset, define total-order invariance as one unique selected ID across all 25 permutations and a maximum-minus-minimum final-estimate range no greater than 1e-12. Define first-rule configuration variation as more than one unique selected ID across its 25 permutations. Define a reported-winner change as at least one first-rule permutation whose optimizer label differs from the total-order optimizer label. Define the preregistered robustness interval, or ROPE, as [-0.005,0.005] balanced-accuracy units around the total-order estimate; a material estimate change occurs when at least one first-rule estimate lies outside that interval. 13. The hypothesis is supported if and only if all 60 datasets satisfy total-order invariance, at least 40 of 60 datasets satisfy first-rule configuration variation, and at least 15 of 60 datasets satisfy reported-winner change. Failure of any one threshold refutes the joint hypothesis. Material-change counts are prespecified corroborating results but do not alter this primary decision. 14. Perform a dataset-level nonparametric bootstrap with seed 987654 and 10000 replicates. In each replicate sample 60 dataset IDs with replacement and recompute the proportions exhibiting first-rule configuration variation, reported-winner change, and material estimate change. Report percentile 95% intervals using numpy.quantile at 0.025 and 0.975 with method='linear'. Bootstrap intervals are descriptive and do not replace the fixed-count decision thresholds. 15. Prespecified secondary analyses are: the number of distinct first-rule selections per dataset; the within-dataset range of first-rule final estimates; the fraction of the 1500 dataset-permutation cases selecting a different ID, optimizer label, or materially different estimate from total order; and sensitivity counts using absolute estimate-change thresholds 0, 0.0025, 0.005, and 0.01. These analyses cannot change the primary conclusion. 16. Save results.csv, dataset_summary.csv, bootstrap.csv, and a PNG named tie_breaking_results.png at 180 dpi. The figure must have two panels: a 60-by-25 heatmap of first-rule selected configuration IDs with optimizer-A IDs colored from one palette and optimizer-B IDs from another, and a bar chart showing the counts of datasets satisfying total-order invariance, first-rule configuration variation, reported-winner change, and material estimate change, with horizontal reference lines at 60, 40, and 15 where applicable. 17. Print the three primary counts and exactly one final label, SUPPORTED or REFUTED, according to Step 13.

Metrics to compute: Total-order invariant datasets: count out of 60 having exactly one selected configuration across 25 permutations and final-estimate range <=1e-12; the required threshold is 60., First-rule configuration-varying datasets: count out of 60 having more than one selected configuration across 25 permutations; the required threshold is at least 40., Reported-winner-change datasets: count out of 60 for which at least one first-encountered permutation selects the opposite optimizer label from the total-order selection; the required threshold is at least 15., Material-estimate-change datasets: count out of 60 with at least one first-rule final estimate differing from the total-order estimate by more than 0.005 balanced-accuracy units., Permutation-level selection-change rate: number of the 1500 dataset-permutation cases whose first-rule selected ID differs from the corresponding total-order ID, divided by 1500., Within-dataset estimate instability: maximum minus minimum first-rule 120-split estimates over the 25 permutations, summarized by median, interquartile range, and maximum., Bootstrap uncertainty: 95% dataset-level percentile intervals from 10000 resamples for the proportions with configuration variation, winner change, and material estimate change.
Expected if hypothesis holds: All 60 datasets will have exactly one total-order configuration and a final-estimate range <=1e-12 across permutations; at least 40 will have multiple first-rule selections; and at least 15 will switch the reported optimizer winner relative to total order. The heatmap will show permutation-dependent configuration and optimizer colors under first encounter while total-order results remain invariant.
Expected if hypothesis fails: The joint hypothesis is refuted if even one dataset fails total-order invariance, fewer than 40 datasets have multiple first-rule selections, or fewer than 15 datasets change the reported optimizer winner; a largely uniform first-rule heatmap and near-zero estimate ranges would indicate that encounter order had little practical effect.
Wall-clock budget: about 20 minutes. Keep the experiment inside it; if
something is slow, reduce sample sizes rather than exceeding the budget.

